## Step 14: bounded Docker smoke and cleanup

Exact smoke command used for both authorized attempts:

```text
python -B -m pytest -p no:cacheprovider --no-cov -q tests/test_pit_optimizer_v2.py -k "policy_worker_docker_smoke"
```

No image pull was allowed or attempted. The locally available digest-pinned image was used with a foreground worker, network disabled, read-only root, one CPU, 256 MiB memory, PID/capability/security limits, one read-only generated policy-package mount, and deterministic synthetic snapshots only.

Attempt 1: `1 failed, 237 deselected in 7.80s`. Read-only image metadata inspection showed that the pinned image already owned the Python entrypoint, while the runner supplied another leading Python token. No provider or replay action occurred. Exact task-name container inspection was empty afterward and the task temporary root was absent. A fake argv regression failed before the change and passed after adding an explicit entrypoint override and corrected command tail.

Attempt 2: `1 failed, 237 deselected in 2.99s`. Worker startup, A-B-A determinism, output, network-none, resource/security limits, user, and exact mount allowlist assertions all passed. The failure occurred only during `client.close()`: stdin EOF had already stopped the worker, Docker KILL returned nonzero, force removal succeeded, and the exact-ID absence query was empty. Post-attempt task-name container and matching process checks were empty, and the generated package was absent.

The cleanup classifier was then narrowed so a nonzero KILL is ignored only when force removal succeeds and an exact-ID absence query conclusively proves absence. Focused fake cleanup matrix: `6 passed, 521 deselected`. Full policy worker/daemon fake group: `44 passed, 483 deselected`.

The smoke also revealed that omitting Windows `SYSTEMDRIVE` from the controller-only Docker environment could make Windows resolve a native cache path under the checkout. The focused fake test was RED with a missing key and GREEN with `1 passed`; `SYSTEMDRIVE` is now passed only to the Docker CLI controller environment and remains absent from the container environment. The exact task-created literal cache directory was permanently removed; it contained only smoke-created Windows cache files, no source or user artifact, and is now absent.

No third Docker invocation was made. Final task-created container list, matching background-process list, generated worker package, and literal cache path are all empty/absent.

